  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>Supported deployment configurations for Lambda in AWS CodeDeploy include <b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes</b>, <b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaAllAtOnce</b>, and <b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes</b>. These are the default strategies:</p><ul><li><b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaAllAtOnce</b>: All incoming traffic is moved to the new Lambda version in a single step. This is the fastest method, but offers no safety net in case of issues.</li><li><b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes</b>: Gradually shifts 10% of traffic every 10 minutes until all traffic is using the new Lambda version. This reduces risk and allows rollback if issues are detected.</li><li><b>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes</b>: Sends 10% of traffic to the new version initially, and if no problems occur, shifts the remaining 90% after five minutes. This is an effective way to catch errors quickly before impacting everyone.</li></ul><p>The options <i>CodeDeployDefault.AllAtATime</i>, <i>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaHalf</i>, and <i>CodeDeployDefault.Immutable</i> are not valid Lambda deployment configurations supported by AWS CodeDeploy. <i>CodeDeployDefault.AllAtATime</i> is not an actual supported configuration for Lambda, <i>CodeDeployDefault.LambdaHalf</i> does not exist, and <i>CodeDeployDefault.Immutable</i> relates to ECS/on-premise deployments, not Lambda. When deploying Lambda functions, choose among the officially supported strategies listed above to ensure smooth rollouts and reduce risk. Tip: Always review AWS CodeDeploy documentation for the latest supported configurations specific to Lambda to avoid confusion between resource types.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your DevOps group is managing the deployment of multiple Lambda functions through AWS CodeDeploy. After setting up applications and relevant deployment groups in CodeDeploy, which deployment configuration strategies are actually supported for Lambda deployments?</p>",
